  "reviewed_by": "Claude Code, implementing the operator's choice that subprojects inherit their parent's Work stages",
  "reason": "Work stages gain inheritance: a subproject without stages of its own uses its nearest parent's, so a portfolio defines stages once on the parent instead of writing them to every subproject (each such write is a project command that starts that project's worker). Core adds WorkService.effective_stages (own stages, else nearest ancestor's, cycle-safe); agent work_list reports the effective list, and Butler's nebula.work.work_stages adds effective_stages and inherited_from while stages/set_work_stages keep meaning the project's own list. The UI uses the effective stages for All projects cells, the stage board, item Stage field and create dialog; a parent with subprojects and no items of its own is a container (no cells, never Missing a stage, no Define stages link); a subproject's board says whose stages it uses, and its editor starts from the inherited keys so saving gives it its own list. Settings catalog, settings-assistant destination and docs describe it; the product index regenerates and verifies. Selected: test_work_stages.py (new inheritance test for the service, agent tool and Butler read), the Butler work-board capability test, test_product_knowledge.py by node ID (docs re-chunk passages; a new assertion finds the inheritance guidance), the stage unit and WorkPage component suites, settings catalog and destination tests, and Playwright area work-stages, whose grep now covers both real-Core LAN journeys (the new one: define stages on a parent, see them on subprojects, container parent, Missing a stage, move a subproject item without writing subproject stages). All 8 profiles passed locally against a LAN Core.",
